Append, never prepend, when Geom2dConvert::ConcatC1 joins a chain's pieces.

What it fixes
  ConcatC1 fuses each piece onto the group built so far with
  Geom2dConvert_CompCurveToBSplineCurve::Add(curve, tolerance), whose
  third argument, After, defaults to false. Add reads After in one place
  only: when the new piece's ends meet both ends of the group, which
  happens only on a chain that closes. There the default makes it
  prepend the last piece, and the concatenation silently starts one
  piece late. A caller that reparametrises the result against a curve
  starting at the first piece is then off by that piece's whole span.
  ShapeUpgrade_UnifySameDomain::UnionPCurves is one: merging the two arcs
  of a closed intersection circle, it gave the new edge pcurves that sat
  a constant 1.233 mm from its 3D circle on both faces (the chord of the
  first arc, 1.673 rad), and the face fix after it rebuilt the smaller
  face around a degenerate edge at its pole.

  The 3D twin, GeomConvert::ConcatC1, already passes true at this step
  (GeomConvert.cxx, "C.Add(Curve1, local_tolerance(j - 1), true)"); this
  brings the 2D copy into line. Left alone, as in 3D: ConcatG1, which
  nothing in OCCT calls; and the closed-group branch, which runs only
  with ClosedFlag set — both UnifySameDomain callers pass false — and
  whose one caller that can reach it, BRepLib::SameParameter through
  C0BSplineToC1BSplineCurve, already restores the origin it moves.

How it was measured
  sphere(8) fused with sphere(1) centred 7.4 mm out at angle a in XY, then
  UnifySameDomain (edges and faces, parcad's 1e-4 tolerances), at every
  whole degree from 0 to 90. The fuse is exact and BRepCheck-valid at
  every a (2145.1397 mm^3). Before: from 6 to 52 degrees the unify
  returns 2140.7807 mm^3, BRepCheck_NotConnected, the bead's face
  unorientable, the merged edge's pcurves 1.657 mm (6) to 0.854 mm (52)
  off its 3D circle; at 53, 54, 55 and 56 degrees it returns a valid solid
  with them 0.759, 0.641, 0.481 and 0.186 mm off (the surface at the
  pcurve against the curve, 9 samples). The window is closed form: the
  bead's kept cap has half-angle arccos(0.557) = 56.2 degrees, so its
  seam cuts the circle only below that, and below 5.96 the big sphere's
  seam cuts it too and the arcs never close. After: valid at every
  angle, 2145.1397 mm^3, deviation 0.000 mm. Called directly, ConcatC1
  returns byte-identical poles before and after for open chains of 2 to
  5 arcs, a closed square and a circle with ClosedFlag set; only closed
  chains move, to start at their first piece. Sixteen beads: 2152.3263
  mm^3 exact against 2144.6606 + 16 x 0.4791 = 2152.3262. parcad's eval
  corpus unchanged but for the case this adds
  (eval/cases/beads-on-a-sphere.json).

Upstream status
  Not yet offered upstream (github.com/Open-Cascade-SAS/OCCT). Still
  present on master as of 2026-09-18.
